Let the given model be $T = aL + bG$, where $T$ is the time in minutes, $L$ is the length of the trail in miles, and $G$ is the altitude gain in feet. We are given two data points:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item When $L = 1.5$ miles and $G = 800$ feet, $T = 69$ minutes. So, $69 = 1.5a + 800b$.
    \item When $L = 1.2$ miles and $G = 1100$ feet, $T = 69$ minutes. So, $69 = 1.2a + 1100b$.
\end{enumerate}
We have a system of two linear equations with two variables $a$ and $b$:
\begin{align} \label{eq:1} 1.5a + 800b &= 69 \\ 1.2a + 1100b &= 69\end{align}
We can solve this system of equations. First, we can multiply the first equation by $1.2$ and the second equation by $1.5$ to eliminate $a$.
\begin{align*} 1.2(1.5a + 800b) &= 1.2(69) \\ 1.8a + 960b &= 82.8 \end{align*}
\begin{align*} 1.5(1.2a + 1100b) &= 1.5(69) \\ 1.8a + 1650b &= 103.5 \end{align*}
Now, we can subtract the first modified equation from the second modified equation to eliminate $a$:
\begin{align*} (1.8a + 1650b) - (1.8a + 960b) &= 103.5 - 82.8 \\ 690b &= 20.7 \\ b &= \frac{20.7}{690} = \frac{207}{6900} = \frac{69}{2300} = \frac{3}{100} = 0.03 \end{align*}
Now, substitute $b = 0.03$ into the first equation:
\begin{align*} 1.5a + 800(0.03) &= 69 \\ 1.5a + 24 &= 69 \\ 1.5a &= 69 - 24 \\ 1.5a &= 45 \\ a &= \frac{45}{1.5} = \frac{450}{15} = 30 \end{align*}
So, $a = 30$ and $b = 0.03$.
Now, we want to find the time it takes to hike a trail that is $4.2$ miles long and ascends $4000$ feet. We can use the model $T = aL + bG$:
\begin{align*} T &= 30(4.2) + 0.03(4000) \\ &= 126 + 120 \\ &= 246 \end{align*}
Therefore, the model estimates it will take $246$ minutes to hike to the top.

We verify the solution by checking the given conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $T = 30(1.5) + 0.03(800) = 45 + 24 = 69$
    \item $T = 30(1.2) + 0.03(1100) = 36 + 33 = 69$
\end{enumerate}
Both conditions are satisfied.

